# Plan-orchestrate protocol

Applies to any task that decomposes into 2+ phases (planning, multi-file
refactors, feature builds, design + implementation work). Skip for
single-step questions, trivial fixes, or pure information requests.

## Why this exists

Opus-tier models are best for planning and judgment but overkill (and
expensive) for executing well-specified file edits, smoke tests,
deploys, and other deterministic work. Doing the entire plan in Opus
costs ~3-5x more than necessary. The fix is a deliberate two-tier
pattern: Opus orchestrates, cheaper models execute.

## Model pins (single source of truth — bump here, not inline)

The rule references named pins below instead of literal model slugs.
When a model gets bumped (new haiku, new sonnet, etc.), update the
pin here and every reference downstream picks it up automatically.

| Pin name             | Current value                  | Used for                                              |
|----------------------|--------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| `<ORCHESTRATOR>`     | claude-4.7-opus (or 4.6)       | Parent turn for plan-class work (Step 1)              |
| `<SCAFFOLDER>`       | composer-2-fast                | Mechanical scaffolding, well-specified file edits     |
| `<EXECUTOR>`         | gpt-5.3-codex                  | Standard implementation phases (Phase 2 of port etc.) |
| `<UI_IMPLEMENTER>`   | claude-4.6-sonnet-medium-thinking | UI work needing judgment + standard implementation |
| `<TEST_WRITER>`      | gpt-5.3-codex                  | Test writing, smoke verification                      |
| `<SAVE_FORMATTER>`   | claude-4.5-haiku-thinking      | /save event drafting (Step 5b hybrid pattern)         |
| `<DOC_DRAFTER>`      | claude-4.5-haiku-thinking      | Drafting documentation from notes                     |
| `<DEEP_REVIEWER>`    | claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking  | Architecture / security review (Step 3 routing table) |

The routing table in Step 3 below is a duplicate view of the same
information for quick lookup during planning. If the two ever drift,
this pin table wins.

## Step 1: Confirm orchestration tier

When the user starts a plan-class request, the parent turn (the
conversation they're in) MUST be Opus 4.6 or 4.7. If it isn't, stop
and tell the user:

> "This is plan-class work. Switch to claude-4.7-opus before I draft
> the plan so the orchestration brain has enough headroom. Sonnet or
> Composer can take it from here once the plan is broken into phases."

Do not silently downgrade. Do not draft the plan in a weaker model and
hope it works. Wait for the switch.

## Step 2: Decompose into named phases

Every plan must produce a numbered phase list before any execution
starts. Each phase declares:

- A specific deliverable (not "investigate" — "produce X file with Y").
- An execution model from the routing table below.
- A boolean: needs_opus_review (defaults false; true only when
  ambiguous trade-offs need to be resolved mid-phase).

Show the phase list to the user before starting Phase 1.

## Step 3: Model routing table

Use these defaults. Pick the cheapest tier the work actually needs.

| Phase type                                                | Model                              |
|-----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| File scaffolding from a clear spec                        | composer-2-fast                    |
| Mechanical refactors / renames across files               | composer-2-fast                    |
| Running smoke tests, lint, build, deploy                  | composer-2-fast or shell subagent  |
| Standard feature implementation (clear spec, low novelty) | gpt-5.3-codex or claude-4.6-sonnet |
| Test writing (unit, integration)                          | gpt-5.3-codex                      |
| Codebase exploration / search-heavy phases                | explore subagent (haiku-tier)      |
| Documentation drafting from notes                         | claude-4.5-haiku-thinking          |
| Tricky debugging across many files                        | claude-4.6-sonnet-medium-thinking  |
| Architecture decisions, trade-off analysis                | parent turn (Opus, no delegation)  |
| Security review, threat modeling                          | parent turn (Opus, no delegation)  |

The parent (Opus) keeps these for itself: planning, reviewing each
phase's output, deciding when to escalate, and final integration.

## Step 4: Spawn each phase as a Task subagent

For phases that delegate, use the `Task` tool with explicit `model:`.
Provide the subagent everything it needs: file paths, expected output,
acceptance criteria, edge cases. Subagents have no memory of the
parent conversation, so context discipline matters.

After each subagent returns, the parent (Opus) reviews the result
before starting the next phase. If something is off, decide: retry the
subagent with more context, escalate to a stronger model, or take it
back to the parent turn.

## Step 5a: /save between phases (auto-enforced)

After every completed phase, run the exocortex `/save` command before
starting the next phase. No exceptions.

This is enforced by a `subagentStop` hook at
`~/.cursor/hooks/auto-save-phase.sh`. When a phase subagent stops, the
hook injects a follow-up message instructing the parent agent to run
`/save`. For the hook to fire, the subagent's `description` argument
to the `Task` tool MUST follow one of these conventions:

- `"Phase N: <short name>"` (canonical, e.g. `"Phase 2: scaffold alerts library"`)
- `"phase-N-<short-name>"` (kebab-case, e.g. `"phase-2-scaffold-alerts"`)

If the subagent description doesn't include a phase marker, the hook
silently does nothing — useful for non-phase subagents (quick lookups,
explore agents, browser automation) that don't warrant a checkpoint.

The save event should name the phase explicitly in its header, e.g.
`"Phase checkpoint — Phase 2: alerts library shipped"`. The hook's
follow-up message will already include this format.

If the save itself fails (vault sync down, etc.), continue with the
next phase but flag it in the running summary; the local event file
will still be created and nightly cron will retry the vault push.

For phases the parent agent (Opus) executes directly without a
subagent, the hook can't fire, so the parent must `/save` manually
before moving on. The rule still applies; the hook is just a safety
net for the delegation path.

## Step 5b: /save model routing (hybrid)

`/save` is expensive on Opus because Step 1 (drafting the event body)
does conversation-pattern reflection. For auto-fired phase saves, use
the hybrid pattern below. For manual saves, daily-end, weekly-review,
and monthly-review, keep them on the parent (those are high-value
reflection events; pay full price for full quality).

### Hybrid /save pattern (auto-fired phase saves only)

1. Parent writes a brief 1-2 line "checkpointing X" line so the user
   sees what is being saved (`show_collapsed` mode). Example:
   `"Checkpointing Phase 2: alerts library (4 files added, 1 commit, smoke tests green). Drafting event via subagent."`

2. Parent spawns a Task subagent for the LLM-heavy event drafting.
   Resolve `<SAVE_FORMATTER>` from the Model pins section at the top
   of this rule (currently `claude-4.5-haiku-thinking`):
   ```
   Task(
     model=<SAVE_FORMATTER>,
     description="phase-save-format",
     prompt="""
       Phase summary: <the 5-bullet inline summary the parent already
       holds in working memory>

       Read these for style and context:
       - git status --porcelain
       - git log --since='6 hours ago' --format='%h %s'
       - the 3 most recent files in .exocortex/events/

       Write the full save event to /tmp/save_event.md using the format
       in .exocortex/commands/save.json step 1.

       CRITICAL FORMATTING RULES:
       - Do NOT include any metadata block at the top of the file.
         No `<!-- Event Metadata -->` header. No `timestamp:`, `machine:`,
         `editor:`, `project:`, or `branch:` lines. The `create_event.sh`
         script adds those automatically — including them in the body
         produces a duplicate metadata block in the saved event.
       - Start the file directly with the `# Phase checkpoint —` header
         on line 1. Nothing before it.

       Required sections after the header:
         - ## What was accomplished
         - ## Decisions made
         - ## Code state
         - ## What's next
         - ## Conversation patterns

       Be specific. Quote real file paths and symbol names from the
       summary.
     """,
   )
   ```

3. Parent runs the shell steps of save.json (`create_event.sh`,
   `sync_event_to_vault.sh`) directly — those are not LLM work, no
   delegation needed.

4. Parent confirms briefly to the user.

Notably this subagent description does NOT match the phase regex
(it is `phase-save-format`, not `phase-N-something`), so the
auto-save hook will not re-fire when this subagent stops. No loop.

### When to bypass the hybrid (use parent for the full /save)

- Manual `/save` requested by the user (they're paying attention).
- `/daily-end`, `/weekly-review`, `/monthly-review` (reflection-heavy).
- The phase being saved involved cross-file architectural decisions
  the haiku-tier model would not capture well.
- The user has explicitly said "use Opus for the save".

## Step 5c: Branching and rollback guidance

**Applies when:** the phase modifies code that ships to production or a
shared environment (any code in `src/`, `workspace/scripts/`,
`packages/*`, deploy configs, migrations, cron jobs, release scripts,
public template files, etc.).

**Skip when:** trivial edits — single-line fixes, comment-only edits,
doc-only edits in `docs/` or `README*`, local notes in `.exocortex/`,
fixture / test data tweaks, or any user-approved fast local change.
These can stay on the current branch when that matches the user's
workflow.

### Recommended branch hierarchy

```
main                              ← stable; only sprint gates merge here
  └── integration/<sprint-name>   ← phase merges land here for sprint integration testing
        └── feature/<area>-<phase>   ← active phase work
        └── fix/<area>-<short-desc>  ← active bug fix
```

For production or team work, prefer creating `integration/<sprint>`
off `main` before the first code-shipping phase. For solo/local repos
without remotes, protected branches, or release gates, the parent may
use the current branch after stating that choice in the plan.

### Branch rules

| Guideline | Who decides |
|---|---|
| For production/team work, start each phase on `feature/<area>-<phase>` or `fix/<area>-<short-desc>` from `integration/<sprint>` | Parent includes this in subagent prompts |
| If no integration branch exists and the repo is solo/local, state whether the phase uses `main`, current branch, or a new branch | Parent |
| Commit and push from subagents only when the user/workflow has approved that behavior and a remote exists | Parent + subagent |
| Subagents do not merge to integration or main | Parent + user handle merges |
| Force-push is never routine; if considered, ask the user first | Parent |
| Tag `main` after meaningful public sprint/release merges | Parent at release gate |

### Rollback ladder (cheapest first)

1. **Discard or abandon the phase branch before merge** — used when a
   phase fails before integration.
2. **`git revert` the merge commit on integration** — preserves history, undoes the change, keeps the phase branch alive for fixing.
3. **`git revert` on `main`** — only when an integration-or-main bug ships. Tag the broken state first (`bad-<sprint>.<n>`) so post-mortem can find it.
4. **Hard reset `main` to a tag** — `git reset --hard v<sprint>.<n>` then force-push. **Forbidden without explicit user approval.** Last resort.

### Phase prompt addition (parent appends to subagent prompt)

```
**Branch discipline:**
- For production/team code, start on `feature/<area>-<phase>` or `fix/<area>-<short-desc>`
- If this is solo/local work, state whether you are using the current branch or a new branch
- Commit/push only if the parent prompt explicitly asks you to and a remote exists
- DO NOT merge to integration or main — parent + user handle that
- DO NOT force-push unless explicitly told

**Rollback awareness:**
- If your phase fails or breaks something: STOP, report what failed, do NOT try to fix forward
- Parent will decide: revert, fix in a follow-up phase, or abandon the branch
```

## Step 5d: Testing quality bar

**Applies when:** the phase adds or changes code that ships to production. Same scope as Step 5c.

**Skip when:** same trivial-edit list as Step 5c.

### What "good tests" means here

| Bar | What it looks like | Anti-pattern to reject |
|---|---|---|
| Unit tests cover the logic, not the framework | Branch coverage on the new helper function | `test_function_exists()` |
| Integration tests hit real boundaries with stubs | Real code path with a fake external client | All-mocks tests that never run the actual code path |
| One smoke test per phase that runs the real path with `DRY_RUN=1` | Real extractor + real poster, dry-run flag prevents external posts | "It compiled" passing for a smoke test |
| External service tests gated behind env flag | `RUN_LIVE_TESTS=1` for Discord / OpenAI / Deepgram tests; default skip in CI | Hard-failing in CI when API key absent |
| Coverage delta is positive | New code: ≥ 80% line coverage in tests added in the same phase | "We'll add tests next phase" |
| Tests written DURING implementation, not after | Scaffolds exist before logic is finalized | Tests bolted on at the end to pass review |

### Recommended acceptance criteria for every code-shipping phase

For production/team work, the phase should not be considered complete
until all relevant items below are true. For small solo/local work, use
the highest-value subset and state what was skipped.

1. ☐ Existing test suite still passes (no regressions)
2. ☐ New tests cover meaningful new code paths (aim for >= 80% line coverage on new files when coverage tooling exists)
3. ☐ At least one smoke check exercises the real code path (with stubs / dry-run for external services)
4. ☐ For pipeline / cron work: a dry-run on the actual deploy environment, results captured in the `/save` event
5. ☐ For domain logic (engines, calculators, scoring): pure-compute unit tests AND a hand-checked example documented as a fixture
6. ☐ For UI work: use an E2E/screenshot/manual smoke check appropriate to the project's tooling

### Phase prompt addition (parent appends to subagent prompt)

```
**Testing requirements:**
- Existing relevant tests should still pass before reporting back
- New production code should usually ship with tests in the same phase
- Coverage on new files: aim for >= 80% line coverage when coverage tooling exists
- Include one smoke check of the real path when practical
- For external services (Discord/OpenAI/Deepgram/etc): gate live tests behind RUN_LIVE_TESTS=1 and skip by default
- Report back the test command + output summary in your final response
```

## Step 6: End-of-plan summary

After the final phase, the parent (Opus) writes one summary covering:

- Each phase, the model it ran on, and the result.
- Any escalations from cheap to expensive models, with the reason.
- Total subagent count and a rough cost-tier estimate (3 cheap + 1
  Opus review beats "all Opus").

This trains the next planning round: if a phase consistently needed
escalation, raise its default model in the routing table.

## When to ignore this rule

- Single-file tweaks or one-line fixes.
- Pure Q&A about existing code.
- The user explicitly says "just do it" or "no plan needed".
- A genuine emergency (production down, security incident) where
  speed beats efficiency.
